            <ArticleTitle>The management of penetrating injuries of the back. A prospective study of 230 patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>72-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This is a prospective study of 230 patients with penetrating injuries of the back. The decision to operate or observe was taken exclusively on the abdominal physical findings. One hundred ninety-five patients (85%) did not require operation, 30 (13%) underwent a therapeutic laparotomy, four (1.7%) an unnecessary operation, and one patient (0.4%) had a completely negative laparotomy. The diagnosis and management was delayed in five (2.2%) patients with no serious consequences. Mortality rates were not recorded in this series. The initial physical examination was accurate in 95.2% of the patients. We suggest that penetrating injuries of the back should be assessed in the same way as anterior abdominal injuries. Physical abdominal examination is reliable in detecting significant intra-abdominal injuries.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
